%%%%%%BOARD 1
https://tinyurl.com/2fy9payr
qx|A1|st||rh||ah|Board 1 IMPs   -   June 26, 2024|pn|hondo717,aspiring,vs_bbo,coxenrider|
md|3SAKQ7HK3DQJT9CJ64,SJ86432HQJ75D32C2,S9HAT862DA6CAKQ97,ST5H94DK8754CT853|sv|0|
mb|1H|mb|P|MB|?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Most players would bid 1@S with my hand. But there is a better style recommended by Marty Bergen that I like. He suggests when you have a game force you bid 2 of a minor first. By doing so you establish a game force right away. And partner can then bid spades if he has them. So that is what I am going to do.|
PG| ***** 1 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BA Kib: ^*nMaybe 3NT?|
PG| ***** 2 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|My answer is no if you mean bidding 3N after 1@H. That takes up way too much room. When you have a game force you want to keep the bidding as low as possible to find the best strain and whether or not to bid slam.|
PG| ***** 3 ***** |


mb|2D|mb|P|mb|3C|sb|SI have reservationS about this bid.|
PG| ***** 4 ***** |


mb|P|mb|3N|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P|mb|PP|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I don't dislike pass by North. And as you can see we should be in slam. So 4@C would be a good bid by North instead of pass.|
PG| ***** 5 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|sb|s|
pc|S4|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I have 5 clubs, 2 hearts, 3 spades, and one diamond for sure. The best play is to take the diamond finesse to guarantee 12 tricks.|
PG| ***** 6 ***** |


