UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That was definitely a hard hand to bid and North/South missed a slam that probably should have been bid. So let me add some additional commentary after the 3@C bid.|
PG| ***** 17 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|After 3@C, South has a bit of a problem. He has 18 |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=bergenstartingpoints,Bergen Starting Points^*N|CP||FP||at| (16 HCP and 1 point for each of this two Quality 3 honor Suits). Slam is still possible. From a probability perspective North will most often have a minimum opener. |

n#|9|at|^^^fIf you do follow the link you can return  HERE  by clicking the  |fp|1|n#|3|at|@L|fp||n^|3|at|   icon button, top left.|
PG| ***** 18 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In that case, their side is probably a little short of the 33 points needed to be in slam. But North could have as much as 20 points - once South bids 2@D to establish a game force, North does not have to jump to show a strong hand. So slam is quite possible.|
PG| ***** 19 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|But what should South bid? He cannot bid 3@S. That is not natural. Since North did not bid 2@S over 2@D, their side cannot possibly have a spade fit. So a 3@S bid here by South would be a stopper ask, since with a spade stopper South should bid 3N.|
PG| ***** 20 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|3N is certainly possible and what South chose. But it does risk missing slam. Another possibility is 4N. That is NOT Blackwood because no fit has been established. It is quantitative. It is asking North to bid 6N with more than a minimum opener, say 16 or more. That works quite well here and is probably a better bid than 3N.|
PG| ***** 21 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|However, some players (incorrectly) think that is RKCB for clubs. If that is the case, then South has no good bid. And even if North correctly interprets 4NT as quantitative, it is not clear what North should consider more than a minimum. As I said, I would bid 6N with 16 or more. But some players might consider bidding 6 with as little as a good 14.|
PG| ***** 22 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Once South makes the conservative 3N bid, what should North do? North has 20 |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=bergenstartingpoints,Bergen Starting Points^*N|CP||FP||at| (17 HCP, one Length Point in hearts and one Length Point in clubs, and one Quality Suit Point). So, opposite the game force that South has shown, slam is still likely. North probably should not pass. |

n#|9|at|^^^fIf you do follow the link you can return  HERE  by clicking the  |fp|1|n#|3|at|@L|fp||n^|3|at|   icon button, top left.|
PG| ***** 23 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|North could bid 4@C. That should be natural, although many players would think it is Gerber. Another alternative is to hope their side does not have 2 quick spade losers and jump to 6@C. That is a choice of slams. Or North could bid 6NT, hoping their clubs will provide the extra tricks necessary to make the contract. In summary, it is possible to get to slam, but very difficult, particularly since some of the possible bids are somewhat ambiguous.|
PG| ***** 24 ***** |


